<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Look at the Fireworks! – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's Festival Season.</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Look at the Fireworks!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Express basic needs and feelings using simple phrases. Interactive: Interact in a predictable pattern with simple questions and answer about familiar things using simple phrases.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe basic needs and preferences related to festival activities using simple sentences (e.g., "I don't like loud drums."). Can ask and respond to predictable questions about festival foods and activities (e.g., "What do you eat at the festival?"). Can describe familiar activities using short, simple phrases (e.g., "I dance!").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts with a "Festival Song and Movement" activity. Play a simple, upbeat festival-themed song. The teacher shows flashcards of key festival items (fireworks, drums, dance, music) and leads students in simple TPR actions (e.g., "Clap!" for music, "Point to the bright fireworks!"). Students say the words chorally as they see the flashcards.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models target phrases and pronunciation. Holding a flashcard of fireworks, the teacher says: "I like fireworks! They are bright! /f/, /f/, fireworks!" (emphasizing /f/ sound). Holding a flashcard of a meal, the teacher says: "I like the meal! Mmm, meal! /m/, /m/, meal!" (emphasizing /m/ sound). Then, showing a picture of a party, the teacher says: "It's a party! /p/, /p/, party!" (emphasizing initial /p/ sound). The teacher models "I like..." and "I don't like..." with different flashcards (e.g., "I like cotton candy!" "I don't like loud drums!").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a picture of fireworks) "Are the fireworks quiet or loud and bright?" (Expected answer: "Loud and bright!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a picture of cotton candy) "Do I like to eat cotton candy or dance with cotton candy?" (Expected answer: "Eat cotton candy!").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "It's Festival Season."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> "My Festival Adventure" - a simple, illustrated picture story or a short, animated video (2 minutes maximum) depicting children at a festival, showing various activities (watching fireworks, eating shaved ice/cotton candy/corn on the cob, listening to music, dancing, seeing balloons, hearing drums). The images clearly match the target vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Express basic needs and feelings using simple phrases. Interact in a predictable pattern with simple questions and answer about familiar things using simple phrases.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> While watching/reading, the teacher pauses and asks simple gist questions for oral responses and encourages pointing.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do you see at the festival?" (Students point to fireworks, balloons, music, etc., and say the words).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are the children happy or sad?" (Expected: "Happy!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do they eat cotton candy?" (Expected: "Yes!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do they dance?" (Expected: "Yes!").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice describing preferences using "I like..." and "I don't like...", asking and answering "What do you like?", and using subject+verb+adjective structures ("It is fun!", "They are bright!"). Vocabulary includes festival, fireworks, meal, shaved ice, cotton candy, music, dance, drums, fun, happy, loud, bright, colorful. Pronunciation focus on /f/, /m/, /p/ sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. "Flashcard Preference Choral Drill":</b> The teacher holds up flashcards of festival items and activities one by one. Students chorally say "I like [item]!" or "I don't like [item]!" based on their preference, followed by a descriptive adjective where applicable. For example, teacher shows "fireworks", students say "I like fireworks! They are bright!" (emphasizing /f/ sound). Teacher shows "loud drums", students say "I don't like loud drums!". Teacher reinforces /m/ in "meal" and /p/ in "party" when those flashcards appear.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. "Sentence Frame Fill-in":</b> The teacher provides sentence frames and prompts. "I like to eat _______." (Teacher shows pictures of cotton candy, shaved ice, corn on the cob). Students choose and complete the sentence aloud, e.g., "I like to eat cotton candy!" or "I like to eat corn on the cob!". Teacher asks: "Is it fun?" and students answer "Yes, it is fun!" or "No, it isn't fun!".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. "Partner Ask & Point":</b> Students work in pairs with a set of mini-flashcards. Student A asks, "What do you like at the festival?". Student B picks a flashcard and says, "I like the [item]!" and adds a simple description like "It is colorful!" or "It is big!". They then swap roles. The teacher circulates, checking for correct use of "I like...", vocabulary, and pronunciation.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "It's Festival Season."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Festival Interview Role-Play". Students work in pairs to interview each other about their festival preferences. Focus on fluency, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme and Scenario:</b> Students imagine they are at a festival and are asking their friend about what they like to see, do, and eat.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are paired up. Each pair receives a "Festival Interview Card" with pictures of festival items/activities (fireworks, shaved ice, cotton candy, music, dance, drums, balloons) and prompts.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A asks Student B questions from the card: "What do you like at the festival?" and "What do you like to eat at the festival?".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B answers using complete, simple sentences based on the pictures or their own preferences, e.g., "I like the fireworks! They are bright and colorful!" or "I like to eat shaved ice. It is yummy!". They can also express negative preferences: "I don't like loud drums." or "I like to dance!".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B can also add a feeling: "It is fun!" or "I am happy!". The teacher can prompt students to talk about cultural festivals using previously learned phrases like "I like Fiestas Patrias" as examples.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After 2-3 questions, they swap roles. The teacher monitors closely, providing support and encouraging interaction.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist. The teacher uses a simple checklist to note students' ability to participate in the "Festival Interview" role-play.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the "Festival Interview" in pairs, asking and answering at least two questions each about festival preferences and activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> Clear enough to be understood (focus on /f/, /m/, /p/).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Uses at least 3-4 festival-related words (e.g., fireworks, cotton candy, dance).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Accuracy:</b> Uses "I like...", "I don't like...", "What do you like?" and simple descriptive sentences (e.g., "It is fun!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Participates actively in the pair interaction.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick "Festival Check" by holding up a green card for "Yes, I can!" or red card for "Not yet!".</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I can say what I like at a festival."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I can ask 'What do you like?'"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite festival word to say today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did you like talking about festivals with your friend? Why?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking lesson? (e.g., specific activities, student engagement with TPR/flashcards).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What challenges did students face with pronunciation or sentence structure? What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for the next lesson to support these areas?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did all students have sufficient opportunities to speak? How can I increase participation for quieter students?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In Lesson 4, students will transition from speaking to writing. They will use the same vocabulary and grammatical structures ("I like...", "It is...") to write simple sentences describing their favorite festival activities and foods, building on the oral practice from today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>